  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MENA Resilience</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Middle East Regional (MER)):  The Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region is one of the most water-scarce regions, globally.  Twelve of its countries fall within the top most water-stressed countries and 60% of its population is exposed to high water stress.  As such, the implications of climate change are especially severe in the region, with impacts including rising temperatures, floods, droughts, and desertification.  These in turn are increasing water, food, and energy insecurity; impacting public health; increasing threats of emerging infectious diseases and agricultural pests; and increased social inequality.  To address these challenges, USAID Bureau for the Middle East and the International Water Management Institute (IWMI) are establishing this cooperative agreement, which will support MENA countries to build resilience to increasing water scarcity, fragility, and climate stressors.  This agreement allows for work on a regional or country-specific scale and addresses the various water security and adaptation needs in the MENA region.  Possible work streams under this agreement will promote cross-sectoral programming, and may include work in the following areas: water management and data-informed resilient water planning; private sector engagement; development and implementation of early warning systems; coherence between food, energy, and water security demands; sustainable governance- polycentric approaches (at multiple levels of government and with multiple public and private actors); nature-based solutions for resilient water, food and biodiversity systems; and promoting transboundary water management.  This activity focuses on translational aspects of science, technology and evidence-based assessments: it will utilize a multidisciplinary approach to tackle water scarcity and adaptation challenges and provide integrated solutions with actionable recommendations.This activity will attribute $485,000 to Global Food Security and $205,000 to Water</narrative>
  </description>
